Subject: fix(run): chown the read-only repo's .git only when the exec user is root

A harness with readonly_repo: true makes the target repo read-only before
the agent starts. Part of that command is chown -R sandbox:sandbox on .git:

  find <repo> -path '*/.git' -prune -o -not -type l -exec chmod a-w {} + &&
    chown -R sandbox:sandbox <repo>/.git && chmod -R u+w <repo>/.git

The chown assumes the exec user is root. OpenShell's Kubernetes driver runs
the sandbox container as a non-root UID (10001) with every capability
dropped, and the sandbox image's sandbox user is a different UID (998). The
chown fails with "Operation not permitted" on every file in .git, and the
run fails:

  Error: read-only repo enforcement failed: exit code 1

So every readonly_repo harness fails on that driver, including review.
Nothing from the agent has run yet at that point. The Docker driver, which
upstream CI uses, execs as root, so this does not show there.

The chown now runs only when the exec user is root. A non-root exec user
already owns the tree it extracted and can chmod it, so the chown would
only fail. The command moves into readonlyRepoCommand, with tests for the
non-root path and the root guard.

The workaround does not cover one limitation. A non-root exec user that
owns the tree can chmod it back to writable, so on that driver read-only is
advisory against the agent. That is for upstream to decide; see the issue
draft.

Breadboard note: this patches Go source compiled into the fullsend binary,
so it belongs to the build list in deploy/scripts/05i-build-fullsend.sh.
Found in the behaviour-test T2 run (BT-B4): the fix-authorization and
fork-bash-routing review scenarios. It is a local workaround; the problem
goes upstream as an issue, not as this patch.

